        ":topic/knowledge-points": [
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186096813,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186096447,
              ":question/id": "2a61b115-4e26-5966-b2a9-d2fedef6b728",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-4282",
              ":question/problem": "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ has magnitude $3$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$.",
              ":question/worked-solution": "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$, we divide $\\mathbf{a}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\mathbf{a}\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "48a66a99-6b4a-5ad5-8922-6f48006ff183",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186096814,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186096493,
              ":question/id": "6dfe09f0-30e2-53bc-a8f6-d7e4a3b8232a",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-5181",
              ":question/problem": "![](images/d3/d3042242136b82b867b8bd7911761ee38318924df0c9979488d20f04117c45d9.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf a$.",
              ":question/worked-solution": "![](images/b3/b316448c885a20a7ed60871ce0f1a4dc4f77bd4658dc6eda3407fb3c89a088cb.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have\n\n$$\n\\angle AHB = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\n, $AH=3$ and $BH=2$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^{2} + BH^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3^{2} + 2^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9 + 4} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$ is $\\sqrt{13}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{\\mid a \\mid} \\cdot a \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "b11642dd-80cc-539c-8c2d-8cdee58e9d3e",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Given Vector in a Diagram"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186096815,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186096448,
              ":question/id": "23d9a601-974b-51c2-a6ea-0ebadde19efc",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-4283",
              ":question/problem": "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $10$, while the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is directed vertically down and has magnitude $20$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a+b}$.",
              ":question/worked-solution": "First, we have to find $\\mathbf{a+b}$. According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$ goes from the tail of $\\mathbf{a}$ to the head of $\\mathbf{b}$:\n\n![](images/32/32c48d8c19fc68fbc172b240ded922f3e2ebc12804411c318ff63fe999aaedcc.png)\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $\\mathbf{a+b}$, we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{a + b}{\\mid a + b \\mid}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, first notice that we obtain a right triangle. So, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a + b & ^{2} &= & a & ^{2} +\\left.\\end{vmatrix}b\\right|^{2} \\\\\n&= 10^{2} + 20^{2} \\\\\n&= 100 + 400 \\\\\n&= 500\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{a+b} &= \\sqrt{500}=10\\sqrt{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{a + b}{\\mid a + b \\mid} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{10\\sqrt{5}}(a + b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "d44ac67b-5e3d-5b1e-bf2c-554b592ba29e",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Finding a Unit Vector Parallel to a Vector Sum"
          }
        ],
