            ":db/id": 17592186097737,
            ":step/content": {
              ":db/id": 17592186097833,
              ":tutorial/content": "A **unit vector** is a vector whose magnitude is equal to $1$.\n\nGiven any vector $\\mathbf a$, we can create a unit vector $\\mathbf u$ by dividing $\\mathbf a$ by its magnitude $\\mid \\, \\mathbf{a} \\, \\mid$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}\n$$\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nthen the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\n![](images/c7/c78d5b75c1d5928e7b269a4a67229487b3d3324e9815b11c5fd1f47f63437d82.png)",
              ":tutorial/id": "a90fd4b3-d41f-589f-9067-39d57e3e8712",
              ":tutorial/math-academy-id": 2906,
              ":tutorial/title": "Introduction"
            },
            ":step/id": "961ff0d0-9cef-5a2d-8207-63481c0cc1ab",
            ":step/math-academy-id": 430121,
            ":step/next": {
